# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Capcom never wrote these rows (their Collection slot is empty/hollow), so the
old fan wording still shows there. This table replaces just the WORDS with new
text in Capcom's style, drafted and reviewed by hand from Capcom's own
reference lines elsewhere in the script.

Same hash-guard discipline as tools/linefix.py / tools/last_rows.py: a row
applies only when the fan's existing unit stream for that (entry, string)
still hashes to what this table was built against, and the replacement is
verified against its own hash before shipping. Either mismatch leaves the row
exactly as it already was.

CONSTRUCTION (not re-derived at build time, only the hash and the final unit
list are baked in here): every replacement keeps the fan row's own non-text
control codes and arguments, in the same order (except the {E050 n}
centring offset of 460/1, 460/2 and 460/23, recomputed as described below) - checked, per row, against
this table's own construction script by comparing the fan row's code sequence
to the new row's code sequence before baking the unit list in. Only the
plain-text units (and, where a widget's fit required it, which whitespace
unit is a line break) differ from the fan's.

The 'hash' is keyed to the fan row as it stands at THIS module's own point in
tools/inject.py's pipeline - after RETITLE (DS[460] only) and after
names.harmonize_entry, both of which already rename a still-kept-fan string's
own character/episode name before this module ever runs (found empirically:
432/245's "John" is already "Shaun", 432/333's "Cameron" already "Aldown",
and every 460 label row's name line already reads Capcom's title, e.g.
"Turnabout Target" already "Turnabout Trigger" - none of that is this
module's own text, and every REWRITE row's 'units' already carries the
POST-rename name so the two halves of the string agree).

460/1 ROUND 2 (same words, a moved line break): "Delete all data and restore /
default settings?" (round 1's wording, same as the fan's own line break)
measured 184 px on line 1 against this dialog's 181 px budget (fan
"Erase...restore...default settings" is 180; swapping in "Delete" alone
pushes it over). Same words, the break moved one word earlier instead:
"Delete all data and / restore default settings?" - 128 px and 171 px (real
MAIN-font advances, the same ruler as every other row here), both within
budget. This is the first of three rows (with 460/2 and 460/23) where the
{E050 n} centring offset is recomputed rather than copied from the fan's row: "restore default
settings?" is now the wider line and keeps offset 0 (as the fan's own wider
line always has), and "Delete all data and" gets a leading {E050 22} -
round((171-128)/2) - to centre it under the wider line, the same additive
pixel model this whole project's line-width tooling already uses for every
{E050 n} on one of these dialogs. Every OTHER code (2xE042, 2xE040, E106)
still matches the fan row's own multiset exactly; only E050's position and
argument - the one thing asked to be recomputed - differ from the fan's.

Rows: 13/1, 13/3, 37/9 (dialogue); 432/245, 432/312, 432/333 (organizer
descriptions); 460/1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23
(save-system prompts); 460/25, 42, 43, 62, 63, 64, 76, 77, 92 (save-slot
episode part labels, "End" -> "Latter").

460/2 and 460/23 recompute their {E050 n} centring the same way as 460/1:
the wider line has no offset and the narrower one gets a leading
{E050 round((wide-narrow)/2)}. 460/2 "Are you sure you want to" 176 px over
"delete all data?" 105 px gives {E050 36} on line 2 (the fan had {E050 7}
on line 1); 460/23 "A new episode" 100 px under "has been added." 112 px
gives {E050 6} on line 1 (the fan had {E050 16} on line 2). 460/3 keeps its
words and codes; only its three fullwidth periods become three U+2025, the
ellipsis unit 460/21 and 460/22 use (same 4 px advance, so line 1 does not
move). 460/8 takes 460/3's layout with "Deleting" -> "Saving" and keeps
both of the fan row's {E040} codes, the first moved after the dots so the
highlight covers "Saving" and the dots as in 460/3. 460/12 and 460/13 are
460/11 with "save" -> "load".
"""
